Ownership and completion proof:
1. Each invocation claims one helper exactly once. The continuation is installed before session/task work. Cancellation before task publication latches under the state lock; it never invalidates a session that is about to create a task. Suspended task creation and publication share that lock. Cancellation after publication captures and cancels only that task, outside the lock.
2. Response/body callbacks use the same lock and a serial delegate queue. Admission errors latch the original error and cancel the task. Later callbacks cannot alter the failure or a completed result. Completion stores Result under the lock; the continuation itself carries only Void.
3. Task completion (or proven no-task startup) precedes finishTasksAndInvalidate. Publication requires both task-completed and session-invalidated flags in either order. Members owning session/task/buffer/continuation are cleared once. The Void wake-up is queued after the final delegate callback on the serial queue; the caller acquires the lock to take Result. No state lock is held across cancel, invalidation, harness callbacks, watchdog cancellation or continuation resumption.
4. The existing dedicated JPIPDeadlineWatchdog enforces absolute elapsed deadlines, including trickling responses. External task cancellation has its own handler. Both cancellation and deadline are rechecked after completion. Already-expired preflight clears unstarted buffer ownership; a second invocation fails its single-use claim without clearing the active invocation.

Storage proof:
Header admission precedes creation of our parsed dictionary and charges exposed Foundation header strings/descriptors, our dictionary and possible channel-ID copying with checked arithmetic. Announced Content-Length is checked against payload and minimum body/result overlap before body storage allocation. Body storage uses Data(count:capacity), tracked capacity independent of received byte count, and checked growth. Each growth admits incoming callback Data.count + old capacity + new capacity plus final old/new result-copy overlap before allocating/copying. A final exact-sized body is copied only after its overlap has been reserved. No raw pointer escapes its scoped Data borrow.
Geometric growth is clamped to min(body policy, floor((storage-header)/2), storage-header-oldCapacity-incomingCount). This reserves room for the final body and avoids repeated exact-size allocation near the limit. Counters record actual accumulation allocations and copied body bytes for deterministic work tests.
URLSession has already allocated callback Data and its exposed HTTP response before invoking us. Those visible owners are checked at callback receipt; we cannot pre-admit their allocation or opaque TLS, networking, cache, queued-callback buffers and allocator overhead. peak is conservative observable response workspace, not total process RSS or a promise to bound all Foundation internals.

